# Interface Composition in Go

One of Go's strengths is the ability to **combine small interfaces** into larger ones. This encourages **modular** design and follows the Interface Segregation Principle.

## Embedding Interfaces

```go
type Reader interface { Read(p []byte) (n int, err error) }
type Writer interface { Write(p []byte) (n int, err error) }
type Closer interface { Close() error }

// Composed interfaces
type ReadWriter interface {
    Reader
    Writer
}

type ReadWriteCloser interface {
    Reader
    Writer
    Closer
}
```

## Interface Segregation Principle

```go
// GOOD: small, focused interfaces
type UserReader interface { Read(id int) (User, error) }
type UserWriter interface { Create(User) error; Update(User) error }

// Compose when needed
type UserRepository interface {
    UserReader
    UserWriter
}

// Functions only depend on what they need
func showUser(repo UserReader, id int) {
    u, _ := repo.Read(id)
    fmt.Println(u)
}
```

## Why Small Interfaces?

1. **Easy to implement** — less surface area
2. **Easy to mock** in tests
3. **Easy to compose** into larger contracts
4. **Clear intent** — each interface has one purpose

starterCode:
package main

import "fmt"

// Small, focused interfaces
type Namer interface {
	Name() string
}

type Describer interface {
	Describe() string
}

type Pricer interface {
	Price() float64
}

// Composed interface
type Product interface {
	Namer
	Describer
	Pricer
}

// Concrete types
type Laptop struct {
	model string
	ram   int
	price float64
}

func (l Laptop) Name() string { return l.model }
func (l Laptop) Describe() string {
	return fmt.Sprintf("%s with %dGB RAM", l.model, l.ram)
}
func (l Laptop) Price() float64 { return l.price }

type Book struct {
	title  string
	author string
	price  float64
}

func (b Book) Name() string    { return b.title }
func (b Book) Describe() string { return fmt.Sprintf("%s by %s", b.title, b.author) }
func (b Book) Price() float64  { return b.price }

// Functions using interface subsets
func printName(n Namer) {
	fmt.Println("Name:", n.Name())
}

func printCatalog(products []Product) {
	for _, p := range products {
		fmt.Printf("%-30s Rp %.0f\n", p.Describe(), p.Price())
	}
}

func totalCost(items []Pricer) float64 {
	total := 0.0
	for _, item := range items {
		total += item.Price()
	}
	return total
}

func main() {
	products := []Product{
		Laptop{"MacBook Pro", 16, 25_000_000},
		Laptop{"ThinkPad X1", 32, 22_000_000},
		Book{"The Go Programming Language", "Donovan & Kernighan", 350_000},
		Book{"Clean Code", "Robert C. Martin", 280_000},
	}

	fmt.Println("=== Product Catalog ===")
	printCatalog(products)

	// Use subset interface
	pricers := make([]Pricer, len(products))
	for i, p := range products {
		pricers[i] = p
	}
	fmt.Printf("\nTotal cost: Rp %.0f\n", totalCost(pricers))
}
